My verdict is **NARROW**: run one revised Stage B0 with proper controls, hold the layer-role probing apart from two cheap checks, and leave Stage C out of this plan. The full report is in `docs/hoplite/c5_layer_plan_2026-11-08/review_OPUS.md`, which is the only file I changed (not committed).

**Sync:** `git fetch` worked and `git pull --ff-only origin main` said "Already up to date". HEAD is `bb8bf702cbff4c73129098d4b660784be289fd75`. I read the whole 618-line review request, the Stage A runner, the EG2 cache script, both Stage A result files (all 23 layers), and D-017 and D-107 to D-111. I ran no training and did not touch test 163, P1 data or `eval/slot_verify.py`.

**What the raw artifacts show**
- **Stage A never fit its training data.** mT5 training CE stayed at 5.16–5.95 after 100 steps on 5 documents. UMT5 training CE was about 8.3, the same as its val CE, so it learned almost nothing. That makes Stage A inconclusive rather than negative: it can't support either "frozen decoder is the bottleneck" or "no content selectivity".
- **The "zero memory" control isn't a fair null.** The runner feeds all-zeros after the adapter's LayerNorm, which a trained adapter never outputs. The large Δzero measures that disruption, not use of memory.
- **The seed changes with the layer** (`seed = 20261108 + layer`), so layer rankings mix layer and seed effects. Layer 19 was the best of 23 noisy estimates on 5 documents, and its Δshuffled is only 0.030; layer 17 had the highest (0.189).
- **mT5 Δshuffled is positive in 18 of 23 layers** (mean about +0.037). The layers share the same 5 documents and pairing, so this is only a weak hint, but "≈0 everywhere" overstates it.
- **The EG2 cache was built with the T5Gemma2 tokenizer.** Because EG2's per-layer embeddings re-inject token identity at every layer, a wrong token ID corrupts that position at every depth. I couldn't measure the mismatch rate: `data/wave2c` and `data/models` are not in this workspace.

| Q | Verdict | Core evidence | Next step |
|---|---|---|---|
| Q1 Probe design | disagree (redesign) | Frequency and numeric labels depend only on the token, so they saturate from layer 0 (D-017: 99.57% / 100%). The within-document permutation control only tests "above chance". | Report every probe against a per-token baseline and layer 0; use type-level control tasks; add probes for recovering nearby tokens and telling documents apart. |
| Q2 Stage A reading | disagree | Underfit training CE; zero control off-distribution; 5 docs, 1 seed. | Record Stage A as inconclusive; rebuild its frozen condition as a control arm inside B0. |
| Q3 B0 single layer | conditional | Layer 19 is a noisy pick tied to its seed. | Keep one pre-registered layer, but make the decoder setup the variable (frozen / LoRA / native encoder / no memory). |
| Q4 B0 DoD | disagree | 20 docs × 100 steps risks a false STOP. The 0.10 threshold has no anchor (frozen layer 17 already hit 0.189). "3 seeds positive" happens 1 in 8 by chance. | Use 300/100 docs, about 900 steps. Primary metric: Δshuffled on target tokens copyable from the source, with ≥5 length-matched shuffles. Gate relative to the native-encoder control. |
| Q5 B1 layers | defer | 5/9/12 were chosen using T5Gemma2 evidence, which D-109 says doesn't transfer. | If B0 passes, pick layers spread by depth, correct for multiple comparisons, and avoid a dense sweep. |
| Q6 B2 models | conditional | UMT5 didn't train under shared settings, so optimisation confounds the model comparison. | Tune learning rate per model and match memory scale; compare selectivity normalised to each model's native control. |
| Q7 Stage C | disagree | Its bar (.45 / 50% / 60%) is far below A1 (.6552 / 100% / 100%), and no resource saving has been shown. | Remove it; any future Stage C needs a new decision record and a non-inferiority test against A1. |
| Q8 Metrics | agree | `grounded` only checks for no new slots, not preservation; bridges failed on preservation (.36). | Add a separate eval-only preservation metric and entity audit, starting with A1; leave the production verifier unchanged. |
| Q9 T5Gemma2 | agree (keep STOP) | D-107/D-108: gap of about 0.25 F1 and 88 points of grounding; repair ceiling .3203. | Don't reopen; any external K/V idea needs a new decision record. |
| Q10 Priority | conditional | B0 decides everything downstream; probes as designed would saturate. | Tokenizer audit first, then revised B0; drop Stage C and the frequency probe first. |

**Top must-fix items**
1. Measure the tokenizer mismatch and re-extract the cache with EG2's own tokenizer for B0. Stage A doesn't need a separate re-run; its frozen condition becomes a B0 control arm.
2. Give B0 four arms: frozen adapter-only, adapter + LoRA, native mT5 encoder + LoRA (positive control), and LoRA with no memory content (negative control). Use the same seeds and data for all, with seeds not tied to the layer.
3. Size B0 at 300 train / 100 val, with val excluding the 5 Stage A documents, and about 900 steps.
4. Measure selectivity on source-copyable target tokens across multiple shuffles, with a bootstrap over documents and seeds. Replace the zero control with a mean-memory control.
5. Pre-register a DoD tied to the native control. If the positive control itself fails, treat that as a broken metric to fix, not a reason to stop.

**Top additional experiments**
1. Tokenizer audit plus per-layer activation scale (about 1 CPU-hour, no training).
2. The revised four-arm B0 (8 CPU-hours or less; hard cap of 10 for the whole plan before a new decision record).
3. A memory knock-out test inside B0: blank the memory at source slot positions and measure the CE change on the matching target tokens. This is the causal link the probes can't provide.
4. Only if B0 passes: the redesigned role probes.

None of these gates are approved; they are reviewer recommendations awaiting a decision record. A1 native stays the fallback reference, and these exploratory results should not be listed alongside it as product numbers.
